\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The paper in one figure: the statistic moves, admission does not, suppression does not follow.} (a) A preservation criterion reasons along one chain, and its levels are operations rather than labels: (P1)/(P2) are $S(d_2)$'s value and the ordering it induces, (P3) what $d_2$ selects, (P4) whether adversarial input enters its aggregate, (P5) max-committed ASR; the first link holds \emph  {by definition}. The two lowest links are the ones we break, each broken arrow annotated with the arm that witnesses it. The lower arc is why they must be broken by intervening rather than comparing, and Mode~S cuts it by construction; the three annotations are three scopes, not one experiment (App.~\ref {app:story}). (b) Mode~S, Krum under model-scaling (§\ref {sec:targeted}): as $\rho $ grows to $54.6$ the \emph  {decision} flips in up to $80\%$ of rounds while the adversarial mass it admits does not, and max-committed ASR stays inside the pre-registered $\pm 0.15$ practical-equivalence margin ($|\Delta |{=}0.026$). Error bars here are Student-$t$ $95\%$ intervals on the levels ($n{=}5$). (c) Six cells, one contrast each, estimated two ways (App.~\ref {app:sixcell}); each connector's length is how much the answer moves when the attenuation channel is closed. The two designs disagree on $4$ of $6$ cells, and on \texttt  {coord\_median}/pixel they have opposite signs, $-0.272$ against $+0.098$. Above the dashed rule are the four cells the withdrawn mechanism was read off, so they are \emph  {training data, not evidence for it}; below are its two out-of-sample cells.}}{2}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:modeS}{{1}{2}{\textbf {The paper in one figure: the statistic moves, admission does not, suppression does not follow.} (a) A preservation criterion reasons along one chain, and its levels are operations rather than labels: (P1)/(P2) are $S(d_2)$'s value and the ordering it induces, (P3) what $d_2$ selects, (P4) whether adversarial input enters its aggregate, (P5) max-committed ASR; the first link holds \emph {by definition}. The two lowest links are the ones we break, each broken arrow annotated with the arm that witnesses it. The lower arc is why they must be broken by intervening rather than comparing, and Mode~S cuts it by construction; the three annotations are three scopes, not one experiment (App.~\ref {app:story}). (b) Mode~S, Krum under model-scaling (§\ref {sec:targeted}): as $\rho $ grows to $54.6$ the \emph {decision} flips in up to $80\%$ of rounds while the adversarial mass it admits does not, and max-committed ASR stays inside the pre-registered $\pm 0.15$ practical-equivalence margin ($|\Delta |{=}0.026$). Error bars here are Student-$t$ $95\%$ intervals on the levels ($n{=}5$). (c) Six cells, one contrast each, estimated two ways (App.~\ref {app:sixcell}); each connector's length is how much the answer moves when the attenuation channel is closed. The two designs disagree on $4$ of $6$ cells, and on \texttt {coord\_median}/pixel they have opposite signs, $-0.272$ against $+0.098$. Above the dashed rule are the four cells the withdrawn mechanism was read off, so they are \emph {training data, not evidence for it}; below are its two out-of-sample cells}{figure.caption.1}{}}
